\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{Twenty hexagons through every edge: a finite lower bound}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{theorem}
There is no finite simple quartic graph of girth six, on at most
thirty-five vertices, having exactly twenty hexagons through every
edge. Consequently $n(4,6,20)\ge36$.
\end{theorem}
This is precisely the lower-bound assertion in the fixed source
Table~2 of Goedgebeur--Jooken, arXiv:2401.08271v2. It gives neither
existence at order thirty-six nor nonexistence at every order.
The printed row also contains $\lambda=19$; that separate clause
is not proved here.

\section{The complete local reduction}
Fix an edge $uv$ of a hypothetical graph. The two trees of depth two
rooted at its endpoints, omitting $uv$ in their first steps, have
twenty-six distinct vertices. An identification or any additional edge
other than an edge between opposite leaves creates a cycle of length
at most five. The nine leaves on either side are divided into three
branches of size three. Between each pair of opposite branches the
cross edges form a matching: two neighbours in one branch would
produce a quadrangle. Each cross edge determines one hexagon through
$uv$, and every such hexagon has this form. Thus there are twenty
cross edges. If the matching sizes are $3-d_{ij}$, then
\[
             0\le d_{ij}\le3,\qquad\sum_{i,j=0}^2d_{ij}=7.       \tag{1}
\]
Exactly seven unfinished leaf incidences remain on each side. Their
endpoints are outside the twenty-six vertices.

We reuse the elementary Moore-tree, root-pair and saturation arguments
of the acquired twenty-two-hexagon component, with the following
seven-incidence specialization. The preceding nonexistence theorem
itself is not a premise.

\begin{lemma}\label{profiles}
For a positive unfinished-degree profile $P$ put
$f(P)=(21-\sum_{s\in P}s^2)/2$. If $P_L,P_R$ are the two profiles,
then necessarily $f(P_L)\le b(P_R)$ and $f(P_R)\le b(P_L)$, where
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrr}
P&1^7&2,1^5&3,1^4&2^2,1^3&3,2,1^2&2^3,1&3^2,1&3,2^2\\\hline
f(P)&7&6&4&5&3&4&1&2\\
b(P)&6&6&6&6&4&5&3&3
\end{array}                                                    \tag{2}
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There are forty hexagons through $u$, since its four incident edges
are each in twenty and each hexagon through $u$ uses two of them.
Twenty contain $uv$. A right leaf of unfinished degree $s$ has
$3-s$ cross neighbours, all in distinct left branches, and supplies
$\binom{3-s}{2}$ of the other twenty hexagons. The missing number
is therefore
\[
       20-\sum_{i=1}^9\binom{3-s_i}{2}
                 =\frac{21-\sum_i s_i^2}{2}=f(P_R).           \tag{3}
\]
These missing hexagons come from pairs of left leaves sharing an
outside neighbour. One outside vertex meets at most three leaves on
one side, at most one per branch. No pair of leaves can be used
twice, since two common neighbours produce a quadrangle.

The bounds $b(P)$ are certified by a separate exact integer
enumeration. Replace a profile by seven distinguished incidence
positions, with the specified repeated leaf names. Partition these
positions by inserting each into every existing part or one new part.
Reject a part with a repeated leaf or more than three leaves, and
reject a leaf pair appearing in two parts. Maximize
$\sum_B\binom{|B|}{2}$ over all remaining partitions. This finite
recursion covers every partition by first appearance of its parts;
the independent standard-library checker obtains exactly the eight
maxima in (2). Ignoring branch restrictions only increases this maximum.
The analogous equation at $v$ proves both coupled bounds.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{pairs}
Let $L_0,L_1,L_2$ be the left branches of leaves and $a_i$ the
cross-edge count incident with $L_i$. Once all left leaves are
saturated, for distinct $i,j,k$ one must have
\[
              \sum_w|N(w)\cap L_i|\,|N(w)\cap L_j|=a_k.        \tag{4}
\]
No further extension can change this sum. The analogous right-side
equations also hold.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $b_{ij}$ counts hexagons using the two root edges in branches
$i,j$, then $a_i+b_{ij}+b_{ik}=20$ and $a_0+a_1+a_2=20$.
Solving gives $b_{ij}=a_k$. The middle vertex of such a hexagon is
a common neighbour of one leaf from each branch, giving (4).
Girth six makes all these paths simple. Saturation fixes all the
leaf neighbourhoods and hence fixes the sum.
\end{proof}

\section{Normalized matchings with checked literal maps}
There are $4\,950$ matrices in (1). Row permutations, column
permutations and transpose give $95$ orbits. Both the Python checker
and the C++ enumerator construct the complete integer matrix set
and check this exact orbit cover. For each representative consider
the bipartite graph on its six branches whose edges correspond to
full matchings. Choose a spanning forest. Relabel leaves independently
within each branch, propagating from a root in every forest component;
then every forest matching becomes the identity. No consistency
condition arises on a forest. These relabellings and the preceding
row, column and transpose operations are actual automorphisms of the
two rooted trees.

Every other block is enumerated as a partial injective map on three
leaves. The respective numbers of choices for deficits $0,1,2,3$ are
$6,18,9,1$. The full normalized product has $1\,415\,940\,318$
choices. The checker visits every child of every surviving prefix;
it discards a prefix only when an added edge closes a quadrangle.
The graph remains bipartite, so this tests all forbidden short cycles.
At a leaf it applies exactly Lemma~\ref{profiles}. Every other leaf
must map to one of the supplied $31\,501$ representative graphs.

The map is not accepted on a canonical certificate alone. Nauty only
proposes a target and a permutation. The verifier checks that the
permutation is bijective on all twenty-six vertices, takes the
unordered root edge to itself, and sends every adjacency row exactly
to the corresponding target row. An absent target or any failed
literal check aborts the verification. Thus a false canonical split
cannot omit a graph, and a false merge cannot establish equivalence.
The list need not be trusted as complete or pairwise nonisomorphic.
All its graphs are also directly checked for the two literal trees,
the cross-edge count, degree bounds and girth.

The exhaustive run visits $214\,143\,141$ block choices, reaches
$56\,703\,540$ girth-admissible leaves and checks literal maps for
$34\,968\,060$ leaves passing (2). These are traversal statistics,
not a count of isomorphism classes of regular graphs. Its output is
a small closed execution receipt; the earlier multi-gigabyte
diagnostic trace is not a verification input.

\section{The first and second outside boundaries}
For each representative partition its seven left incidences by their
actual outside neighbour. Inserting the next incidence into every
eligible existing part or one new part covers every partition.
A part has no repeated leaf, at most one leaf from each branch, and
no leaf pair already used in another part or sharing an old neighbour.
These exclusions are exactly necessary quadrangle obstructions.
During insertion, the new pairs between branches may not exceed
the nonnegative target in (4); at the end they must equal it.
Apply the same independent procedure on the right. Right attachments
cannot change the left leaf neighbour sets, so these tests are sound
before the two sides are combined.

There are $15\,498$ surviving side partitions across all targets;
only $93$ targets have a surviving partition on both sides. Combine
a left and a right partition by choosing a partial one-to-one
matching between their parts, with combined part size at most four.
Every outside vertex of a full extension consists of one part from
each side or a part from just one side. Conversely no two distinct
parts on the same side may be merged, since they denote different
vertices. This gives complete coverage. All original twenty-six
vertices are now saturated.

The joining stage has $133\,152$ choices. Direct girth checks reject
$125\,408$; every other choice has a checked literal isomorphism
to a supplied joined graph, with the same permutation and adjacency
tests as before. These maps need not preserve a root, because all
following tests concern the entire partial graph. The $7\,744$
survivors use $262$ supplied targets of orders $32,33,34$.

For each target first count simple alternate length-five paths through
each edge. A count greater than twenty is a permanent obstruction:
adding vertices or edges cannot remove those hexagons. This rejects
$120$ targets. For every other target let its deficient vertices be
the old boundary. Enumerate every subset of possible edges between
old boundary vertices, rejecting degree overflow or distance less
than five before insertion. All such edges of an actual completion
occur in one of these choices.

Next list all remaining incidences at old vertices. Their new
endpoints are again enumerated by first appearance of parts, with
no repeated old vertex, degree at most four, and no short cycle.
At most $35-n$ new vertices may be used when the old target has
order $n$. In particular more than $4(35-n)$ incidences are impossible.
This is an order bound on necessary extensions, not an assertion
that every partial extension has a full regular completion.
The empty incidence list is included.

Every old vertex is now saturated. No edges among new vertices,
or farther outside, are chosen. They are unnecessary because of the
following acquired elementary observation.
\begin{lemma}\label{saturation}
If every vertex at distance at most two from $x$ in a partial
quartic graph $B$ has degree four in $B$, every hexagon through
$x$ in any quartic completion is already contained in $B$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every edge of a hexagon through $x$ has an endpoint reached from
$x$ along that hexagon in at most two steps. Such an endpoint is
saturated, so the edge is already present. Equivalently no first
edge leaving $B$ can start within this saturated radius-two ball.
\end{proof}
The complete boundary search has $7\,778$ old-edge choices and
$1\,466$ girth-admissible incidence extensions within the order cap.
For every one, the verifier finds an edge $xy$ with the radius-two
saturation property at $x$, counts all simple alternate length-five
paths from $x$ to $y$, and obtains a value different from twenty.
Lemma~\ref{saturation} makes this disagreement permanent. If even
one extension lacks such a rejection, verification fails.

\begin{proof}[Proof of the theorem]
A hypothetical graph of order at most thirty-five gives the Moore
trees, a normalized cross-matching leaf, two first-side partitions,
their partial matching, and one enumerated second-boundary extension.
Every normalization is an actual permutation, and every later reuse
is checked against every adjacency row. The terminal obstruction
persists under all undecided farther edges. Since all cases reject,
the hypothetical graph cannot exist.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Verification and attribution.}
Root supplied the initial diagnostic programs, the target reduction,
and the previously accepted twenty-two-hexagon geometry. The present
author assembled the complete streamed coverage verifier, independent
integer coverage and capacity checks, literal-map guard, closed
execution interfaces and this explicit bounded-order proof.
The diagnostic targets are a candidate provider only. Pynauty/nauty
are not classification-completeness premises: every used permutation
is directly verified, including root preservation where required.
Compilation, runtime and numerical controls are recorded separately.
Goedgebeur and Jooken's fixed original lower bound and their witness
constructions retain their attribution. No new graph count,
whole-source recovery, named-conjecture, Lean or measurement claim
is made by this component.
